This case impressively shows that, although rare, SCA14
is an important differential diagnose in cases with a mostly
cerebellar and slowly progressive phenotype, especially if
there is a positive family history and other hereditary forms
of ataxia have been excluded. Finally, we show evidence
that exclusive mutation screening of exon 4 of PRKCG
might conceal a considerable number of SCA14 cases.
